1
00:00:04,810 --> 00:00:09,050
So, what's the big news you had us rush all the way over here for?

2
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:13,810
Our news. My company has asked me to head up our office in Tulsa.

3
00:00:14,010 --> 00:00:16,560
So as of Monday, I'm being officially relocated.

4
00:00:16,650 --> 00:00:18,310
Oh, my God!

5
00:00:16,650 --> 00:00:18,310
What? Monday?

6
00:00:18,450 --> 00:00:21,820
How long do you have to go for?

7
00:00:18,450 --> 00:00:21,820
They said it could be up to a year.

8
00:00:21,860 --> 00:00:24,350
A year?

9
00:00:21,860 --> 00:00:24,350
Well, do you have to go?

10
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:29,020
Well, I kind of have to, don't I?

11
00:00:29,230 --> 00:00:32,130
Because of this stupid thing.

12
00:00:34,970 --> 00:00:38,600
There's nothing like the support of your loving wife, huh?

13
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:41,070
Wait a minute. Wait, you can't go to Tulsa.

14
00:00:41,270 --> 00:00:45,170
Maybe you forgot, but we have tickets to the Jets game next week!

15
00:00:45,410 --> 00:00:48,110
I'm sorry, but I don't think I'll be able to make it.

16
00:00:48,610 --> 00:00:51,480
But we were gonna go see the Jets!

17
00:00:53,310 --> 00:00:57,680
You can't go. I mean, you're the glue that holds this group together.

18
00:00:58,190 --> 00:01:00,620
Really?

19
00:00:58,190 --> 00:01:00,620
Not you.

20
00:01:04,240 --> 00:01:05,940
I can't believe you guys are moving.

21
00:01:08,930 --> 00:01:11,440
I call their apartment!

22
00:01:08,930 --> 00:01:11,440
No!

23
00:01:50,830 --> 00:01:53,300
Here you go, Rach.

24
00:01:50,830 --> 00:01:53,300
Thank you, Joey.

25
00:01:53,370 --> 00:01:56,410
You know what? I'm not even sure I can have caffeine.

26
00:01:56,780 --> 00:02:00,520
I went through this with Ben and Carol. One coffee won't affect your milk.

27
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:04,110
Just to be sure, I'm gonna call Dr. Wiener.

28
00:02:08,580 --> 00:02:10,630
Every time?

29
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:14,900
You don't have to call him whenever you have a question, okay?

30
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:18,350
Trust me. I know this.

31
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:18,350
All right. I trust you.

32
00:02:20,150 --> 00:02:22,650
I can see you dialing. I don't understand why...

33
00:02:22,740 --> 00:02:25,520
I'm on the phone! Dr. Wiener?

34
00:02:27,750 --> 00:02:32,110
It's so weird seeing Ross and Rachel with a baby. It's just so grownup.

35
00:02:32,270 --> 00:02:35,640
I know, yeah. I feel like we're all growing up.

36
00:02:36,830 --> 00:02:40,380
A person named "Wiener." God, that kills me.

37
00:02:40,540 --> 00:02:44,860
Look at you all grownup.

38
00:02:40,540 --> 00:02:44,860
Well, actually, you know what?

39
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:46,850
I am, you know?

40
00:02:46,860 --> 00:02:50,300
Well, that whole thing with Rachel made me realize...

41
00:02:50,460 --> 00:02:53,480
...that maybe I'm ready for a more serious relationship, you know?

42
00:02:53,570 --> 00:02:57,360
Like, I'd like to meet a nice, mature, commitment-minded lady.

43
00:02:57,460 --> 00:03:00,020
And looks aren't as important as...

44
00:03:00,850 --> 00:03:03,030
Nah, she's gotta be hot.

45
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:05,530
You know, I might know somebody.

46
00:03:05,910 --> 00:03:08,520
How about you set me up with someone, and we double-date?

47
00:03:08,670 --> 00:03:10,850
I can do that. Yeah. How's Friday?

48
00:03:10,890 --> 00:03:12,460
Done!

49
00:03:10,890 --> 00:03:12,460
All right.

50
00:03:12,510 --> 00:03:15,010
Good, really? Okay, let's see.

51
00:03:15,460 --> 00:03:16,830
All right.

52
00:03:18,610 --> 00:03:21,250
You know who's great? Sandy Poopack.

53
00:03:22,510 --> 00:03:23,970
"Poopack"?

54
00:03:27,050 --> 00:03:31,520
Yeah. All right. Well, that rules out Lana Titwyler.

55
00:03:35,580 --> 00:03:38,840
I've got good news.

56
00:03:35,580 --> 00:03:38,840
You got out of the whole Tulsa thing?

57
00:03:39,860 --> 00:03:41,680
Okay, I have news.

58
00:03:42,970 --> 00:03:46,730
You don't have to move to Tulsa. You can stay here and keep your job.

59
00:03:46,840 --> 00:03:48,200
That's great! How?

60
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:51,850
My boss and I worked out a deal where I'm in Tulsa four days a week.

61
00:03:51,870 --> 00:03:54,400
So the other three, I can be here with you.

62
00:03:54,730 --> 00:03:56,780
So you're gonna be gone four days a week?

63
00:03:58,940 --> 00:03:59,730
No.

64
00:04:00,280 --> 00:04:03,780
I'm sorry, are you just used to saying that?

65
00:04:05,850 --> 00:04:09,240
No. I can't be away from you for that long.

66
00:04:09,460 --> 00:04:11,500
Really?

67
00:04:09,460 --> 00:04:11,500
Yeah, you're my husband.

68
00:04:11,570 --> 00:04:15,930
I won't live in a different state than you for 208 days out of the year.

69
00:04:16,100 --> 00:04:20,670
That's fast math. We could use you in Tulsa.

70
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:27,130
Thanks for trying to figure out a way, but if you go to Tulsa, I go with you.

71
00:04:28,710 --> 00:04:30,680
You said that without gagging.

72
00:04:31,020 --> 00:04:31,980
I know!

73
00:04:35,770 --> 00:04:37,640
Well, excuse me?

74
00:04:37,830 --> 00:04:40,690
Oh, yeah? Well, up yours too!

75
00:04:42,690 --> 00:04:46,040
Who the hell was that?

76
00:04:42,690 --> 00:04:46,040
Dr. Wiener.

77
00:04:47,940 --> 00:04:51,200
Rach, you can't call people at 3:00 in the morning.

78
00:04:51,210 --> 00:04:54,410
You know what? You sound just like his wife.

79
00:04:55,140 --> 00:04:57,170
Was there anything even wrong with Emma?

80
00:04:57,240 --> 00:05:00,810
Yes, of course there is! Okay? I'm not insane.

81
00:05:02,180 --> 00:05:03,390
What was it?

82
00:05:03,920 --> 00:05:05,280
Hiccups.

83
00:05:06,420 --> 00:05:10,380
I told you, you can't call him every time any little thing comes up.

84
00:05:10,580 --> 00:05:14,330
Well, not anymore I can't. He fired us!

85
00:05:16,330 --> 00:05:19,410
What are we gonna do? We have to find a pediatrician.

86
00:05:19,580 --> 00:05:22,630
Wait, Monica said that when you guys were growing up...

87
00:05:22,630 --> 00:05:24,860
...you liked your doctor. What was his name?

88
00:05:24,950 --> 00:05:27,200
Dr. Gettleman?

89
00:05:27,470 --> 00:05:29,910
Yeah, no, I don't think that's a good idea.

90
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,430
In fact, I think he's dead.

91
00:05:32,550 --> 00:05:34,860
Why does everything happen to me?

92
00:05:36,270 --> 00:05:39,670
I promise, first thing tomorrow, we'll find another doctor.

93
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:41,820
But I gotta get up early, and I'm not feeling well.

94
00:05:41,850 --> 00:05:44,980
You're not feeling well? What do you have? Rubella?

95
00:05:45,050 --> 00:05:47,990
Because don't go near Emma, she has not had that shot.

96
00:05:48,530 --> 00:05:52,620
You know, come to think of it, it does feel rubella-like.

97
00:06:02,370 --> 00:06:05,330
Wiener!

98
00:06:05,540 --> 00:06:07,430
Wiener! Wiener! Wiener!

99
00:06:07,560 --> 00:06:08,530
Rachel!

100
00:06:09,050 --> 00:06:12,170
Great! Now he's gonna know it was me!

101
00:06:18,250 --> 00:06:21,770
So how is this for our big double date tonight?

102
00:06:23,210 --> 00:06:25,210
Oh, my God.

103
00:06:26,390 --> 00:06:29,920
Great. Just the reaction I was hoping for.

104
00:06:30,030 --> 00:06:33,710
Yeah, so you found someone for me? You didn't forget?

105
00:06:33,810 --> 00:06:36,360
Of course not. And you'll love Mary Ellen.

106
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:40,090
She's really smart and cute and funny and...

107
00:06:40,170 --> 00:06:45,600
I can't tell you how I know this, but she is not opposed to threesomes.

108
00:06:46,560 --> 00:06:47,860
All right.

109
00:06:48,270 --> 00:06:52,040
So tell me something about my guy.

110
00:06:48,270 --> 00:06:52,040
No.

111
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:57,270
Come on, give me something. What's his name?

112
00:06:58,070 --> 00:07:01,070
Mike.

113
00:06:58,070 --> 00:07:01,070
Mike. Okay, what's his last name?

114
00:07:01,090 --> 00:07:04,680
Damn it! Is there no mystery left in romance anymore? !

115
00:07:05,560 --> 00:07:08,720
We'll see you and Mike at the restaurant in a couple hours.

116
00:07:08,770 --> 00:07:12,860
All right, great. See you. Bye-bye.

117
00:07:13,100 --> 00:07:16,840
Why'd I have to say Mike? I don't know a Mike! Why couldn't I have said...?

118
00:07:19,710 --> 00:07:22,770
There's no guys in there!

119
00:07:25,010 --> 00:07:28,930
It's impossible to find a good doctor. How do you know the good ones...

120
00:07:29,060 --> 00:07:31,860
...from the ones who'll push their penis against your knee?

121
00:07:33,920 --> 00:07:34,990
Excuse me?

122
00:07:35,250 --> 00:07:37,450
I know what she's talking about.

123
00:07:42,460 --> 00:07:44,980
Well, we've gotta find a new pediatrician.

124
00:07:45,270 --> 00:07:48,450
Ross was getting sick last night, and Emma may have caught it.

125
00:07:48,620 --> 00:07:51,250
Why don't you see Dr. Gettleman?

126
00:07:48,620 --> 00:07:51,250
Ross said he died.

127
00:07:51,530 --> 00:07:54,460
He didn't die. I just saw his daughter last week.

128
00:07:54,700 --> 00:07:58,270
She said he was fine. Her, on the other hand, botched Botox.

129
00:08:00,560 --> 00:08:02,980
Great. Well then, I'm gonna take Emma to see him.

130
00:08:03,340 --> 00:08:04,970
I wonder why Ross said that he died?

131
00:08:05,120 --> 00:08:07,960
Maybe he confused him with his childhood therapist.

132
00:08:08,200 --> 00:08:09,110
He saw a therapist?

133
00:08:09,380 --> 00:08:12,490
He used to have a recurring nightmare. It freaked him out.

134
00:08:12,560 --> 00:08:14,020
Wow, what was it?

135
00:08:14,920 --> 00:08:16,310
That I was going to eat him.

136
00:08:30,800 --> 00:08:31,700
Mike!

137
00:08:34,200 --> 00:08:34,970
Yeah?

138
00:08:38,580 --> 00:08:39,510
Okay.

139
00:08:44,790 --> 00:08:46,970
I can't believe I'm doing this with you.

140
00:08:47,160 --> 00:08:49,680
Although I did just end a nine-year relationship...

141
00:08:49,790 --> 00:08:52,010
...so I should be open to taking some risks.

142
00:08:52,120 --> 00:08:54,050
Everything is gonna be fine. Follow my lead, okay?

143
00:08:54,080 --> 00:08:56,080
All you have to do is pretend to be Mike.

144
00:08:56,130 --> 00:08:58,010
I am Mike.

145
00:08:56,130 --> 00:08:58,010
Attaboy.

146
00:09:01,040 --> 00:09:02,600
Here they come.

147
00:09:03,030 --> 00:09:06,090
I'm Phoebe.

148
00:09:03,030 --> 00:09:06,090
Phoebe. Mike. How you doing?

149
00:09:06,180 --> 00:09:08,300
Nice to meet you.

150
00:09:06,180 --> 00:09:08,300
Joey, this is Mary Ellen Jenkins.

151
00:09:08,550 --> 00:09:12,170
So, Mike, how do you and Joey know each other, anyway?

152
00:09:14,910 --> 00:09:17,450
How do Joey and I know each other?

153
00:09:17,720 --> 00:09:20,560
If I had a nickel for every time somebody's asked me that.

154
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:25,430
From school.

155
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:25,430
We met in college.

156
00:09:27,590 --> 00:09:28,880
I mean high school.

157
00:09:31,660 --> 00:09:35,670
You guys go way back, then. So, what are you up to these days?

158
00:09:35,830 --> 00:09:39,530
Well, I'm a lawyer.

159
00:09:35,830 --> 00:09:39,530
Mike, attorney at law!

160
00:09:40,400 --> 00:09:42,760
Actually, I just gave up my practice.

161
00:09:40,400 --> 00:09:42,760
What?

162
00:09:43,020 --> 00:09:45,430
That's the kind of thing you usually run by me.

163
00:09:47,820 --> 00:09:51,710
I want to play piano professionally. If I don't do this now, I never will.

164
00:09:51,900 --> 00:09:54,260
Great. I like that better than the lawyer thing.

165
00:09:54,570 --> 00:09:57,210
Which is why I waited until now to introduce you to Mike.

166
00:09:58,810 --> 00:10:00,810
You thought he was still a lawyer.

167
00:10:00,900 --> 00:10:04,120
No, that's not what I meant. Let's get you a cocktail.

168
00:10:09,090 --> 00:10:10,080
What are you doing?

169
00:10:10,160 --> 00:10:12,460
Looking for restaurant jobs for you in Tulsa.

170
00:10:12,560 --> 00:10:15,260
That's so sweet. Did you find anything?

171
00:10:15,310 --> 00:10:17,900
Slim Pickins.

172
00:10:15,310 --> 00:10:17,900
Nothing, huh?

173
00:10:17,910 --> 00:10:20,750
No. Slim Pickins. It's a barbecue joint.

174
00:10:22,300 --> 00:10:25,680
They're looking for a cook. Actually, "cook" may be a bit of a stretch.

175
00:10:26,040 --> 00:10:28,870
They're looking for somebody to shovel mesquite.

176
00:10:29,990 --> 00:10:32,480
"Slim Pickins"? That is so cheesy.

177
00:10:32,570 --> 00:10:34,210
Well, So Cheesy also has an opening.

178
00:10:35,590 --> 00:10:38,740
Honey, that's okay. I know this woman, Nancy...

179
00:10:38,900 --> 00:10:41,620
...who's a restaurant biz headhunter. She may know something.

180
00:10:41,770 --> 00:10:44,590
Can I say how much I appreciate you coming with me?

181
00:10:44,670 --> 00:10:48,270
When we get to Tulsa, I am taking you for a great dinner at Slim Pickins.

182
00:10:50,220 --> 00:10:51,280
So Cheesy?

183
00:10:53,150 --> 00:10:54,280
Whole Hog?

184
00:10:56,270 --> 00:10:58,730
It's gonna be tough to keep kosher in Tulsa.

185
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,730
Hi, Nancy? Hi, it's Monica Geller.

186
00:11:07,050 --> 00:11:11,260
I'm good. Listen, I'm looking for a job in Tulsa.

187
00:11:12,810 --> 00:11:15,310
Yeah, well, my husband's been relocated.

188
00:11:16,730 --> 00:11:18,040
Because I love him.

189
00:11:19,530 --> 00:11:21,530
No, I don't want a job in New York.

190
00:11:22,300 --> 00:11:24,750
Javu's looking? Oh, my God!

191
00:11:25,660 --> 00:11:28,850
He asked for me personally? Oh, my God!

192
00:11:29,850 --> 00:11:32,940
Wow, this is really, really flattering.

193
00:11:33,570 --> 00:11:35,900
But I'm moving to Tulsa.

194
00:11:36,370 --> 00:11:39,070
So, just, if you would tell Javu...

195
00:11:39,160 --> 00:11:40,890
...l'll take it!

196
00:11:43,660 --> 00:11:46,180
My name's Rachel Greene. I have an appointment for Emma.

197
00:11:46,250 --> 00:11:49,610
Dr. Gettleman is finishing up with a patient. He'll be out shortly.

198
00:11:49,610 --> 00:11:50,500
Okay, thank you

199
00:11:50,500 --> 00:11:53,980
I think you just have a cold. It's definitely not strep.

200
00:11:54,220 --> 00:11:55,720
Thanks, doctor.

201
00:11:58,280 --> 00:12:00,850
Would you like a lollipop?

202
00:11:58,280 --> 00:12:00,850
Do you even have to ask?

203
00:12:07,560 --> 00:12:09,020
He is alive!

204
00:12:15,660 --> 00:12:19,570
It's so surprising that you and Joey have known each other for so long...

205
00:12:19,650 --> 00:12:21,130
...and I've never heard about you.

206
00:12:21,340 --> 00:12:25,420
That's because we had a bit of a falling out.

207
00:12:25,880 --> 00:12:28,850
Mike hit my mom with a car.

208
00:12:31,350 --> 00:12:35,120
No, I didn't.

209
00:12:31,350 --> 00:12:35,120
That's okay. I have forgiven you.

210
00:12:35,600 --> 00:12:37,720
Now we're friends again, and everything's great.

211
00:12:38,120 --> 00:12:39,690
Well, wait. Is your mom okay?

212
00:12:39,890 --> 00:12:43,080
Please, we're trying to have a conversation.

213
00:12:44,930 --> 00:12:47,090
You're a lot nicer on Days of Our Lives.

214
00:12:47,300 --> 00:12:49,860
Days of Our Lives! That's why you look so familiar!

215
00:12:49,990 --> 00:12:51,130
What?

216
00:12:49,990 --> 00:12:51,130
What?

217
00:12:51,340 --> 00:12:53,430
What?

218
00:12:53,860 --> 00:12:56,700
Do you not know each other?

219
00:12:58,080 --> 00:13:03,610
Of course we do. He's playing a game we used to play in high school.

220
00:13:03,720 --> 00:13:07,020
We pretend we don't know each other. We played all kinds of games.

221
00:13:07,080 --> 00:13:10,840
You remember that one where I punch you in the face for not being cool?

222
00:13:13,290 --> 00:13:16,780
Let me ask you something: How many sisters does Joey have?

223
00:13:17,230 --> 00:13:17,960
Six.

224
00:13:18,290 --> 00:13:20,080
What are you doing? I said seven!

225
00:13:23,910 --> 00:13:27,110
Joey, why did you set me up with a stranger?

226
00:13:27,490 --> 00:13:31,530
Because I forgot about our date. I'm so sorry.

227
00:13:31,620 --> 00:13:35,380
I'm sorry too. And just to be clear, I didn't hit his mother with a car.

228
00:13:36,470 --> 00:13:41,460
You are unbelievable. I spent so much time finding the perfect girl for you.

229
00:13:41,590 --> 00:13:44,700
Mary Ellen is really smart and cute and loose.

230
00:13:46,710 --> 00:13:48,110
Who are you kidding?

231
00:13:54,400 --> 00:13:56,810
You find some guy off the street for me?

232
00:13:56,910 --> 00:13:59,030
God, this is humiliating!

233
00:13:59,270 --> 00:14:03,380
I'm so sorry. If you don't like this guy, I can find you a better one.

234
00:14:03,470 --> 00:14:04,490
Mike? Mike?

235
00:14:07,040 --> 00:14:08,160
I'm out of here.

236
00:14:08,710 --> 00:14:10,250
It was nice meeting you.

237
00:14:10,890 --> 00:14:14,290
You're leaving too?

238
00:14:10,890 --> 00:14:14,290
I'll stay if you can tell me my name.

239
00:14:14,350 --> 00:14:15,710
Have a good night.

240
00:14:19,740 --> 00:14:22,560
We leave tomorrow, and you still have a lot to pack.

241
00:14:22,800 --> 00:14:23,750
You're right.

242
00:14:24,900 --> 00:14:26,240
Maybe I shouldn't go.

243
00:14:27,560 --> 00:14:28,320
What?

244
00:14:30,270 --> 00:14:34,570
So Nancy told me about this job at this great restaurant, Javu?

245
00:14:35,020 --> 00:14:37,120
But it's just a little outside of Tulsa.

246
00:14:37,180 --> 00:14:38,460
Well, how far outside?

247
00:14:39,410 --> 00:14:40,370
Manattan?

248
00:14:42,920 --> 00:14:44,840
And you're thinking of taking it?

249
00:14:47,900 --> 00:14:51,420
Before, you said that being with me was more important than any job.

250
00:14:51,440 --> 00:14:56,340
But I guess now it's old job, me, new job.

251
00:14:57,780 --> 00:14:59,850
I'm gonna miss this hand!

252
00:15:02,220 --> 00:15:04,240
I know this is a lot to ask...

253
00:15:04,280 --> 00:15:07,010
...but, my God, this is a once-in-a-lifetime opportunity.

254
00:15:07,120 --> 00:15:10,060
What happened to "you can't live without me four days a week"?

255
00:15:10,140 --> 00:15:14,260
Well, if you really think about it, I mean, four days is not that long.

256
00:15:14,440 --> 00:15:17,770
I see you Monday before you go to work...

257
00:15:17,860 --> 00:15:19,630
...and Thursday when you get back...

258
00:15:19,670 --> 00:15:23,290
...and I always work late on Tuesdays, so really if you think about it...

259
00:15:23,430 --> 00:15:24,710
...it's really just one day.

260
00:15:25,020 --> 00:15:30,010
And well, if we can't make it one day, we got real problems, my friend.

261
00:15:34,270 --> 00:15:37,460
I think you should take the job.

262
00:15:34,270 --> 00:15:37,460
Really?

263
00:15:37,690 --> 00:15:41,410
I know it must be important to you when you start chattering like a monkey.

264
00:15:43,880 --> 00:15:46,290
That's the nicest thing anybody's ever said to me.

265
00:15:51,010 --> 00:15:54,760
How was the pediatrician?

266
00:15:51,010 --> 00:15:54,760
I really liked him.

267
00:15:54,850 --> 00:15:58,340
Yeah, yeah. It was really, really, really good.

268
00:15:59,890 --> 00:16:02,640
You promised not to say anything.

269
00:15:59,890 --> 00:16:02,640
I know.

270
00:16:03,360 --> 00:16:06,080
Ross still sees his pediatrician!

271
00:16:08,300 --> 00:16:09,890
I don't care.

272
00:16:11,390 --> 00:16:14,820
Are you serious? You still see Dr. Gettleman?

273
00:16:14,860 --> 00:16:17,100
He is a brilliant diagnostician!

274
00:16:17,170 --> 00:16:20,100
Diagnostician or boo-boo fixer?

275
00:16:21,430 --> 00:16:24,330
Seriously, you gotta go to an appropriate doctor.

276
00:16:24,410 --> 00:16:28,850
Why? I know it's a little weird, but he is a great doctor, okay?

277
00:16:28,870 --> 00:16:30,750
He knows my medical history.

278
00:16:30,830 --> 00:16:33,460
And every time I go in there, he makes a big deal.

279
00:16:33,520 --> 00:16:36,200
You know, "Look, it's my favorite patient."

280
00:16:36,240 --> 00:16:39,140
Does he say that before he sticks his thermometer in your tushy?

281
00:16:40,580 --> 00:16:44,880
I seem to remember someone bringing his security blanket to college.

282
00:16:44,960 --> 00:16:47,900
That was not a security blanket. That was a wall hanging.

283
00:16:48,010 --> 00:16:50,540
It didn't spend much time on the wall!

284
00:17:00,470 --> 00:17:01,520
Excuse me.

285
00:17:03,040 --> 00:17:06,500
Hi. I was hoping to run into you. Can we talk?

286
00:17:06,640 --> 00:17:07,360
Sure.

287
00:17:08,230 --> 00:17:10,760
Look, I'm sorry, really. I'm so embarrassed.

288
00:17:15,760 --> 00:17:20,300
Really, I'm a pretty nice guy. Just ask my parole officer.

289
00:17:23,430 --> 00:17:25,960
Apparently, I'm not a funny guy.

290
00:17:28,720 --> 00:17:31,890
Well, why did you go along with that?

291
00:17:32,010 --> 00:17:36,210
Because I was told that I'd get a free dinner, which I didn't.

292
00:17:36,230 --> 00:17:40,840
And that I'd meet a pretty girl, which I did.

293
00:17:43,020 --> 00:17:44,020
That's true.

294
00:17:46,620 --> 00:17:51,000
Well, is anything you told me about yourself true?

295
00:17:51,080 --> 00:17:53,380
My name is Mike. And I do play piano.

296
00:17:53,430 --> 00:17:58,100
Prove it.

297
00:17:53,430 --> 00:17:58,100
There isn't a piano here.

298
00:17:58,180 --> 00:18:00,240
That wouldn't stand in the way of a true pianist.

299
00:18:02,480 --> 00:18:03,530
Okay.

300
00:18:20,580 --> 00:18:22,580
You are really good.

301
00:18:25,030 --> 00:18:30,070
I play a little guitar myself.

302
00:18:25,030 --> 00:18:30,070
Really? That's great.

303
00:18:30,230 --> 00:18:34,060
What kind of music do you play?

304
00:18:30,230 --> 00:18:34,060
Like acoustic, folksy stuff, you know?

305
00:18:34,080 --> 00:18:37,140
But right now I'm working on a couple Iron Maiden covers.

306
00:18:40,330 --> 00:18:45,150
Do you think that, maybe, sometime, I could...

307
00:18:46,200 --> 00:18:48,540
It's okay. Go ahead, ask me out.

308
00:18:49,860 --> 00:18:53,580
Okay. You think, maybe, sometime I could take you out?

309
00:18:53,910 --> 00:18:55,890
You just caught me off guard.

310
00:18:57,330 --> 00:18:59,280
Yeah, that would be nice.

311
00:18:59,860 --> 00:19:02,960
Look at this! My two best friends!

312
00:19:07,980 --> 00:19:10,570
Excuse me. I don't mean to be a jerk...

313
00:19:10,640 --> 00:19:13,990
...but the baby with the rash came in after me.

314
00:19:15,080 --> 00:19:17,800
The doctor will be right with you, sir.

315
00:19:15,080 --> 00:19:17,800
I hear you...

316
00:19:21,660 --> 00:19:23,640
Mommy, I can't find Waldo.

317
00:19:24,260 --> 00:19:26,740
What, the circus? He's behind the elephant.

318
00:19:26,850 --> 00:19:31,040
Wow. So your child's a big fan of the Waldo books too?

319
00:19:32,730 --> 00:19:34,500
Yeah, that's how I know.

320
00:19:36,480 --> 00:19:38,870
I'm Ross, by the way.

321
00:19:36,480 --> 00:19:38,870
Hi, I'm Sally.

322
00:19:39,750 --> 00:19:43,280
So no ring. Can I assume you're also a single parent?

323
00:19:43,510 --> 00:19:47,560
I am a single parent.

324
00:19:43,510 --> 00:19:47,560
It's hard, isn't it?

325
00:19:47,730 --> 00:19:51,290
There's no time for a social life. Where are you gonna meet someone?

326
00:19:52,850 --> 00:19:57,710
Let's say, I don't know, you met someone in the pediatriciars office.

327
00:19:58,820 --> 00:20:01,840
Rossie? We're ready for you.

328
00:20:11,390 --> 00:20:12,850
Yeah...

329
00:20:13,440 --> 00:20:16,670
Come on, Ross Jr. It's time to go in.

330
00:20:18,940 --> 00:20:20,090
Mommy.

331
00:20:24,860 --> 00:20:27,000
Mommy, what's wrong with that man?

332
00:20:27,080 --> 00:20:29,590
Hey, I helped you find Waldo!

333
00:20:37,370 --> 00:20:39,840
Good, you haven't left yet.

334
00:20:37,370 --> 00:20:39,840
Where have you been?

335
00:20:39,900 --> 00:20:43,380
I got held up at Dr. Gettlemars office.

336
00:20:43,990 --> 00:20:47,720
There was some guy that freaked everybody out.

337
00:20:48,600 --> 00:20:51,320
You got here just in time. I really have to go, buddy.

338
00:20:51,360 --> 00:20:52,140
Oh, man.

339
00:20:52,240 --> 00:20:56,630
Promise to call me when you land?

340
00:20:52,240 --> 00:20:56,630
Of course I will. I love you.

341
00:20:56,890 --> 00:20:58,720
I love you too.

342
00:21:02,960 --> 00:21:06,670
Watch the tongue, people. We got a baby over here.

343
00:21:07,610 --> 00:21:09,980
Bye, Chandler.

344
00:21:07,610 --> 00:21:09,980
Bye.

345
00:21:10,590 --> 00:21:12,800
Bye, honey.

346
00:21:10,590 --> 00:21:12,800
Bye.

347
00:21:17,260 --> 00:21:20,630
What's the matter, Joe?

348
00:21:17,260 --> 00:21:20,630
I'm mad at you for leaving.

349
00:21:21,330 --> 00:21:23,840
You know, you're nothing but a big leaver.

350
00:21:25,570 --> 00:21:28,350
A big leaver with a stupid suitcase.

351
00:21:30,740 --> 00:21:34,690
Any chance you're trying to pick a fight to make all of this easier?

352
00:21:38,310 --> 00:21:41,040
Dude, you see right through me!

353
00:21:50,280 --> 00:21:53,180
Okay, well... Bye, Mon.

354
00:21:53,510 --> 00:21:56,060
Bye, Ross. Rachel.

355
00:21:56,180 --> 00:21:58,710
Bye, Emma.

356
00:21:56,180 --> 00:21:58,710
Okay, bye-bye.

357
00:21:59,310 --> 00:22:03,380
Have a good trip. Okay. Oh, my God.

358
00:22:05,570 --> 00:22:07,990
Wait.

359
00:22:09,970 --> 00:22:12,350
It goes old job...

360
00:22:13,220 --> 00:22:14,430
...new job...

361
00:22:15,500 --> 00:22:16,550
...and you.

362
00:22:18,790 --> 00:22:20,600
This is just something I have to do.

363
00:22:21,320 --> 00:22:22,830
I know.

364
00:22:23,850 --> 00:22:25,810
I love you so much.

365
00:22:26,240 --> 00:22:28,150
I know that too.

366
00:22:40,580 --> 00:22:42,860
Don't worry. I'll be back before you know it.

367
00:22:44,120 --> 00:22:46,200
Yes, it will be the same.

368
00:22:46,870 --> 00:22:49,260
Because I know, that's how.

369
00:22:49,550 --> 00:22:50,760
I promise.

370
00:22:50,970 --> 00:22:52,120
You double promise?

371
00:22:56,870 --> 00:22:58,270
Call me when you land.

372
00:22:59,940 --> 00:23:02,390
Can I talk now?

373
00:22:59,940 --> 00:23:02,390
Okay, bye.

374
00:23:02,560 --> 00:23:04,280
Joey!

375
00:23:02,560 --> 00:23:04,280
He had to board.

